In Java (especially in Streams and Optional), map vs flatMap is a very common interview topic. The core difference is how they handle nested data.

🔹 1. map()

Definition:
Transforms each element into another single value.

👉 Output size = same as input (1 → 1 mapping)

Example:
List<String> names = List.of("Ali", "Ahmed");

List<Integer> lengths = names.stream()
    .map(String::length)
    .toList();

👉 Result: [3, 5]

Key Idea:
One input → one output
No flattening
Used for simple transformation
🔹 2. flatMap()

Definition:
Transforms each element into a stream (or collection) and then flattens them into a single stream.

👉 Output can be multiple values per input (1 → many)

Example 1: Nested Lists
List<List<String>> list = List.of(
    List.of("A", "B"),
    List.of("C", "D")
);

List<String> result = list.stream()
    .flatMap(Collection::stream)
    .toList();

👉 Result: [A, B, C, D]

Example 2: Split words into characters
List<String> words = List.of("Hi", "Bye");

List<String> chars = words.stream()
    .flatMap(word -> Arrays.stream(word.split("")))
    .toList();

👉 Result: [H, i, B, y, e]

🔥 Key Difference (Important for Interview)
| Feature      | map()            | flatMap()                         |
| ------------ | ---------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Mapping type | 1 → 1            | 1 → Many                          |
| Output       | Stream of values | Flattened Stream                  |
| Nested data  | Keeps nesting    | Removes nesting                   |
| Use case     | Simple transform | Flatten collections / nested data |

💡 Simple Way to Remember
map() → wraps
flatMap() → unwraps / flattens
⚡ Interview Trick Question
What happens if you use map() instead of flatMap()?
list.stream().map(Collection::stream)

👉 Output:

Stream<Stream<String>>   ❌ (nested stream)

But with flatMap():

Stream<String>           ✅ (flattened)
🔥 Real-world Use Case (Very Important)
Fetch users → each user has multiple orders
You want all orders in a single list
users.stream()
     .flatMap(user -> user.getOrders().stream())
     .toList();